---
/**
 * Brief behind the "Debt refinancing" chain row on /risk/. Backstory is dated
 * text. Where we stand and the levels in What we're watching are built from
 * the row's feeds at build time (src/lib/briefs.ts), so they move with every
 * feed push. The long essay that lived here is in docs/archive/pages/risk/.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import RiskWatch from '../../components/RiskWatch.astro';
import { debtRefiBriefNow } from '../../lib/briefs';
import '../../styles/risk.css';

const title = 'Debt refinancing';
const subhead = 'Treasury has to sell more debt at auction. When buyers bid less, yields rise and the cost reaches mortgages. The numbers below come from the /risk/ watch-list feed.';

const brief = debtRefiBriefNow();

const watchItems = [
  ...brief.watch,
  "Foreign official holdings of Treasuries in TIC Table 5, near $3,773.1 billion at the end of July 2026, and the dollar's share of world currency reserves in IMF COFER data, near 57.13% in the first quarter of 2026.",
];

const sources = [
  { text: 'U.S. Treasury, Debt to the Penny', href: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/' },
  { text: 'Congressional Budget Office, The Budget and Economic Outlook', href: 'https://www.cbo.gov/publication/61882' },
  { text: 'U.S. Treasury, Monthly Statement of the Public Debt, 31 Aug 2026 (PDF)', href: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/static-data/published-reports/mspd-entire/MonthlyStatementPublicDebt_Entire_202608.pdf' },
  { text: 'U.S. Treasury, most recent quarterly refunding documents', href: 'https://home.treasury.gov/policy-issues/financing-the-government/quarterly-refunding/most-recent-quarterly-refunding-documents' },
  { text: 'U.S. Treasury FiscalData, Treasury Securities Auctions Data', href: 'https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/treasury-securities-auctions-data/auctions' },
  { text: 'Freddie Mac 30-year fixed mortgage rate, via FRED (MORTGAGE30US)', href: 'https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/MORTGAGE30US' },
  { text: 'U.S. Treasury, TIC Table 5, major foreign holders of Treasury securities', href: 'https://ticdata.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/tic/Documents/slt_table5.html' },
  { text: 'IMF, Currency Composition of Official Foreign Exchange Reserves (COFER)', href: 'https://data.imf.org/cofer' },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} - Rycode`}
  description={subhead}
  variant="risk"
>
  <article class="risk-digest risk-brief">
    <header class="risk-digest-head">
      <h1 class="risk-digest-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="risk-digest-subhead">{subhead}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="risk-digest-main">
      <section class="risk-digest-section" id="backstory">
        <h2>Backstory</h2>
        <p>On 17 Sep 2026, Treasury's Debt to the Penny data put debt held by the public, the part owed to investors outside the government, near $32.39 trillion. Total public debt was near $40.09 trillion. The Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects debt held by the public rising from about 101% of GDP, the economy's yearly output, in 2026 to about 120% in 2036, with rising interest costs a main reason deficits grow.</p>
        <p>Inflation can make old fixed-rate debt lighter, because it is repaid in dollars that buy less. That is not a clean reset. About $2.15 trillion of Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) and about $0.68 trillion of floating-rate notes were outstanding on 31 Aug 2026, and their payments rise with inflation or rates. If investors think rates are being held down to ease the government's bill, they can demand more yield to hold long bonds, so the debt that rolls over gets more expensive.</p>
        <p>Savers pay the other side. When prices rise faster than what cash and savings earn, households lose buying power. The row on /risk/ follows the refinancing path: how much Treasury borrows, how strongly buyers bid at auction, and what that does to mortgage rates.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="risk-digest-section" id="where-we-stand">
        <h2>Where we stand</h2>
        <p class="risk-brief-asof">{brief.asOf}</p>
        <p>The row tracks one chain. Treasury borrows, buyers bid for the new debt at auction, and the yields they demand pass through to borrowing costs like mortgages.</p>
        <p><strong>Step 1, Borrow.</strong> {brief.borrow}</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2, Auctions.</strong> {brief.auctions}</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3, Costs.</strong> {brief.costs}</p>
        <p>{brief.strain}</p>
      </section>

      <section class="risk-digest-section" id="watching">
        <h2>What we're watching</h2>
        <RiskWatch items={watchItems} />
      </section>

      <section class="risk-digest-section risk-digest-sources" id="sources">
        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          {sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <a href={source.href}>{source.text}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .risk-brief .risk-digest-main {
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .risk-brief .risk-brief-asof {
    margin-top: -0.35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--digest-faint, rgba(248, 250, 252, 0.4));
  }
</style>
